.\"	@(#)fseek.3s	6.3 (Berkeley) 2/24/86
.\"
.TH FSEEK 3S  "February 24, 1986"
.AT 3
.SH NAME
fseek, ftell, rewind, fgetpos, fsetpos \- reposition a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B fseek(stream, offset, ptrname)
.br
.SM
.B FILE
.B *stream;
.br
.B long offset;
.PP
.B long ftell(stream)
.br
.SM
.B FILE
.B *stream;
.PP
.B rewind(stream)
.PP
.B int fgetpos(stream, pos)
.br
.SM
.B FILE
.B *stream;
.br
.B fpos_t *pos;
.PP
.B int fsetpos(stream, pos)
.br
.SM
.B FILE
.B *stream;
.br
.B const fpos_t *pos;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fseek
sets the position of the next input or output
operation on the
.IR stream .
The new position is at the signed distance
.I offset
bytes
from the beginning, the current position, or the end of the file,
according as 
.I ptrname
has the value 0, 1, or 2.
.PP
.I Fseek
undoes any effects of
.IR  ungetc (3S).
.PP
.I Ftell
returns the current value of the offset relative to the beginning
of the file associated with the named
.IR stream .
It is measured in bytes on UNIX;
on some other systems it is a magic cookie,
and the only foolproof way to obtain an 
.I offset
for
.IR fseek .
.PP
.IR Rewind ( stream )
is equivalent to
.IR fseek ( stream ,
0L, 0),
and clears the error indicator as well.
.PP
.I Fgetpos
stores the current position in
.RI * pos ,
and
.I fsetpos
returns the stream to a position
.I fgetpos
recorded earlier on the same file.
.B fpos_t
is a
.B long
here, holding the offset alone, because every stream on this system is a
byte stream with no conversion state to carry beside it; a portable program
uses the pair rather than
.I ftell
and
.I fseek
because an implementation whose offsets do not fit a
.B long
has only this one.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
lseek(2),
fopen(3S),
ungetc(3S)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Fseek
and
.I fsetpos
return \-1 for improper seeks, otherwise zero.
.I Fgetpos
returns \-1 when the position cannot be determined, and zero otherwise.
